    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d684">

                <pb facs="0977" n="967" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg967"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.93/8–1045</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President
                        Truman and the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-08-10">10 August 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>M–25257. While at Potsdam General Marshall<anchor n="17" xml:id="d684-f1"/> and
                    Admiral King<anchor n="18" xml:id="d684-f2"/> told me of the proposed landings
                    in Korea and Dairen if the Japanese gave in prior to Soviet troops occupying
                    these areas.</p>
                <p>Considering the way Stalin is behaving in increasing his demands on Soong I
                    recommend that these landings be made to accept surrender of the Japanese troops
                    at least on the Kwantung Peninsula and in Korea. I cannot see that we are under
                    any obligation to the Soviets to respect any zone of Soviet military
                    operation.</p>
                <p>General Deane<anchor n="19" xml:id="d684-f3"/> concurs.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d684-f1" n="17" xml:id="d684-f1-content">
                <p>General of the Army George C. Marshall, Chief of Staff, U. S. Army.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d684-f2" n="18" xml:id="d684-f2-content">
                <p>Fleet Adm. Ernest J. King, Commander in Chief, United States Fleet and Chief of
                    Naval Operations.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d684-f3" n="19" xml:id="d684-f3-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. John R. Deane, Chief of the U. S. Military Mission in the Soviet
                    Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
